>...I suspect there's a problem with
>externalities -- e.g. if I own a smoky factory do I have the right to
>pollute the air, or if an oil tanker do I have the right to unleash a
>slick?

The principle of pollution as tresspass clarifies this.  It all comes
down to who owns the air or water.  No one has the right to pollute
the property of another.

>  For the natural rights to be truly objective they should be as
>readily distinguishable from fictions as odd from even numbers.

Maybe.  I think ordinal measurement can be just as objective as cardinal.

>The statists' power is enhanced through persuading people to see positive
>pseudo-rights as objective, and ancient negative rights and liberties as
>the talk of capitalist dogs.

Precisely, all this based upon a market-failure theory of history, which
is why we must counter with better explanation of the failures that have
occurred in the past.

>But
>supposing we have a goal-based or ancient liberties interpretation of
>natural rights:
>
>(i)  When humans are free to pursue their own chosen goals, they are led
>by "an invisible hand", a spontaneous order, to promote unintended ends,
>which are frequently in the best interests of human society.
>
>(ii)  A set of objective natural rights exist, inherent in the nature of
>mankind, which following discovery will, if enforced, increase general
>prosperity.  The two characteristics of natural rights are negativity and
>universality.  Negativity means the right not to be interfered with, as
>opposed to positivity, which is the right to force others to provide you
>with goods or services.  An infinite number of rights are awaiting
>discovery.  Universality means these rights apply to everybody.
>
>It looks like (ii) is just a more longwinded way of saying (i), which is
>already known by supporters of free markets.

(ii) is more concrete than (i).  That's helpful to those who aren't very
good at abstraction.

>You gave the example of the Dutch becoming prosperous after enforcing 
>natural rights, which could possibly be used as evidence.  If human nature
>may be such that a set of natural rights exist which promote prosperity if
>enforced or respected, how could this be falsified if rights were never
>enforced or respected?  If I claimed that a genie existed who could
>dramatically increase human prosperity if he wanted to, critics would
>remark: "How convenient it is for the theory that he never chooses to!"

I see: there's a methodological difference here.  I would argue that the
fact that rights-enforcement leads to increased prosperity in pareto-
optimal fashion can be deduced from praxaeological axioms plus a few
explicit empirical assumptions, even if there had never been a society
in which rights had been enforced.  This would be unfalsifiable, but I
don't think the requirement of falsifiability applies here.

>>>Hardly
>>>anyone even thinks about enforcing their right to non-coercion or right
>>>to life, and statists certainly don't respect these.
>>
>>This is an issue of abstraction.  Since most people don't think abstractly
>>about these things, but concretely, they think of concrete rights.
>
>We have to find some way of encouraging abstract thinking, and show how
>intangible concepts can lead to tangible benefits.

True, but we also have to express ourselves more concretely, and show how
intangible statist concepts can lead to tangible losses.

>>...natural rights were known to the Scholastics during
>>the Crusades in great clarity and detail, but little use was made of this
>>knowledge until the Dutch broke free of the Spanish Empire - proceeding to
>>become one of the most prosperous societies in the world upon the enforcement
>>of natural rights as they understood them.
>
>Was this during the 1590s or after 1648?  My encyclopedia mentions how the
>Dutch economy blossomed during the 1590s after the Spanish had been
>defeated in many sea battles and were forced to call a truce.  But it
>wasn't until 1648 that the Netherlands' independence was recognised in the
>Treaty of Munster.  Reasons given for the 1590s prosperity are the Baltic
>trade and herring fishery.  If they were able to enforce natural rights
>over this period it would support the theory, also if things worked out
>well in the late 1600s.  They did have a war to fight (with the British
>as allies) against the French, but in 1713 the Treaty of Utrecht was said
>to have concluded the war and also the period of the Netherlands'
>greatness as a world power.

The Dutch revolted against the Spanish Inquisition, to esablish a large
degree of religious freedom, as well as against the high taxes of the
Spanish Empire, to establish lower taxes.  Thus, in throwing off the
Spanish, they enforced freedom of conscience and property rights, long
before official formal recognition of their independence.  Spain had
been prosperous and productive, too, until the Inquisition drove out
the human capital of the Jews and Moriscos (Saracen converts to Catholi-
cism).

