Subject: [#94-3-112] LANG: Limits and implied consent

There was a Wall Street Journal article a while ago about electronic
business meetings--conducted by email with everyone in the same room.
It seemed to work very well, since people didn't need to make territorial
displays to get heard.

There was mention of a business that was renting time in electronic
conference rooms for companies that didn't want to build their own.
Does anyone know if anything came of the idea.

At first, I thought that the idea of having everyone in the same
room was not-getting-the-point-of-the-technology silliness. Now,
I'm agnostic--maybe there are subliminal effects of being in other
people's presence, even if the interactions are indirect.

(I've been reading Hall's _Beyond Culture_. He mentions that, if
people are talking with each other, their brain waves go into
sync. The only thing that breaks the synchronization is one
person being called out of the conversation by a third. Maybe
people who like phone conversation are those who can go into 
sync from a purely audio input? I'd be willing to bet that
email from separate rooms does *not* promote syncing.)

